## 2. Architecture and concurrency model
Two programs: Agent (TCP server on port @@PORT@@) and Controller (client). TCP carries the line-based
control protocol and the raw PUT/GET bytes; UDP carries periodic monitoring datagrams from the Agent to a
port opened by the Controller.
Concurrency: **thread-per-connection** (pthreads, detached). Justification: each session has independent
state (auth, buffer, monitor stream) so blocking I/O in a dedicated thread keeps the code simple and
isolates slow clients (e.g. a long upload) from others. Alternatives considered: fork() (heavier, needs
shared log), select/poll/epoll (scales better but needs explicit per-connection state machines for
PUT/GET). Thread count is not a concern for the required ≥5 clients.
`[DIAGRAM: draw Controller ⇄ Agent (accept loop → session thread → monitor thread) → UDP back to Controller]`

